1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
The instruction set architecture is the part of a processor that a compiler is allowed to see.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Four things are visible: the operations, the registers, the instruction formats and the addressing modes.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Three things are not visible, and they are the ones people wrongly include: cache size, clock frequency, cache levels.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:20,000
GATE 2025 Set 2 asked exactly this, and only the register count was the correct option.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Two processors with the same instruction set can differ in every implementation detail.

6
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So when a question says architecture, read the contract, not the hardware.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Every instruction is a fixed number of bits, and the fields must spend them exactly.

8
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:40,000
A register field costs log base two of the number of registers; an opcode costs log base two of the number of instructions.

9
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:45,000
GATE 2014 Set 1: a 32-bit word, 64 registers, 45 instructions, two register operands.

10
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Six bits for the opcode, twelve for the two registers, so fourteen bits are left for the immediate.

11
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:55,000
An unsigned 14-bit immediate reaches 16383, not 16384, because counting starts at zero.

12
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:00,000
GATE 2025 Set 1 gave 64 registers and 50 instruction types with one register operand: 20 bits remain.

13
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
A fixed opcode field wastes space, because most instructions need fewer operands than the widest one.

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:10,000
The escape trick: one opcode value means read the next four bits as another opcode.

15
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Fifteen codes give three-register instructions, one escape gives fifteen two-register ones, and again for one register.

16
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The codes must be prefix-free: no code may be the beginning of another, or the decoder cannot tell them apart.

17
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
Fifteen sixteenths plus fifteen two-hundred-fifty-sixths plus fifteen four-thousand-ninety-sixths is just under one.

18
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:30,000
GATE 2020 made it two formats: eight six-bit I-type opcodes taken, and all sixteen four-bit R-type opcodes free.

19
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:35,000
The same expression costs a different number of instructions on each machine.

20
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Three-address: three instructions, one per operation, every operand a register.

21
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Two-address: five, because the destination is also a source and needs a copy first.

22
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:50,000
One-address, the accumulator machine: seven, because every value must pass through one register.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
Zero-address, the stack machine: eight, because each operand is pushed and each result popped.

24
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Fewer addresses per instruction means shorter instructions and more of them.

25
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:05,000
An addressing mode is a recipe that turns bits in the instruction into one address.

26
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:10,000
Immediate puts the value in the instruction; register puts it in a register. Neither touches memory.

27
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:15,000
Direct puts the address in the instruction; indirect puts the address of the address there.

28
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Displacement adds a constant to a base register; indexed adds an index register, scaled by element size.

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
PC-relative adds the offset to the program counter, which is what makes a branch position-independent.

30
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Autoincrement uses the register, then advances it by the size of the item, not by one.

31
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Count the memory accesses, and count the instruction fetch too.

32
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Immediate and register cost nothing beyond the fetch; direct and register indirect cost one read.

33
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Memory indirect costs two, because the pointer must itself be fetched.

34
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:50,000
A two-word instruction costs two fetches, and its address field sits in the second word.

35
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:55,000
Indexed costs one read plus an addition; the addition is time, not a memory access.

36
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:00,000
So the same instruction can be charged four or five, depending on which question is asked.

37
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:05,000
Each mode is in the instruction set because one construct of a high-level language needs it.

38
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
Immediate exists for constants; indirect exists for pointers, which are addresses held in memory.

39
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:15,000
Base with index exists for array elements; base with displacement exists for a field of a record.

40
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:20,000
GATE 2026 Set 1 asked for this matching, and the record field was base with displacement.

41
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:25,000
GATE 2017 Set 1 put a struct in a register and asked for one field.

42
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The distinction is the scaling: an array multiplies the index by the element size, a record does not.

43
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:35,000
A branch stores a distance, not an address, and the distance is measured from the next instruction.

44
00:03:35,000 --> 00:03:40,000
GATE 2017 Set 1: four-byte instructions, the branch is the fourth, the target is the first.

45
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:45,000
The next instruction is sixteen bytes past the target, so the offset is minus sixteen.

46
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:50,000
Write it as a signed number: half the field range is spent on backwards jumps.

47
00:03:50,000 --> 00:03:55,000
Absolute addresses break when the program moves; a base register or a PC offset survives.

48
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:00,000
GATE 1998 and GATE 2004 both asked which modes relocate; base and relative do, absolute does not.

49
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:05,000
In a load-store machine, only load and store touch memory.

50
00:04:05,000 --> 00:04:10,000
GATE 2026 Set 1 asked for Z equals X plus Y: two loads, an add and a store.

51
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:15,000
The tempting wrong answers put one memory operand inside the add.

52
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:20,000
RISC means register-to-register arithmetic, a fixed instruction length and a hardwired control unit.

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,000
GATE 2018 asked which of those three are RISC characteristics, and all three are.

54
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:30,000
CISC does the opposite, at the cost of a harder decoder.

55
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:35,000
Step one: write the format as a sum of field widths and set it equal to the word length.

56
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:40,000
Step two: use the ceiling of log base two for every field, and never round down.

57
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Step three: for a mode question, compute the effective address from the given state, then read once.

58
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Step four: for a branch, subtract the next instruction address, and keep the sign.

59
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:55,000
The five traps: the fetch, the scaling, indirect as one read, an unsigned offset, the cache.

60
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Every one of those five has cost marks in a real paper.

